#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120884} :activity/id #uuid "dcdc8ecc-c5d8-56ba-b664-0870beb832b2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words:\n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n$$L = \\left\\{ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=3x \\right\\}$$\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 3x\\end{aligned}],$ where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above.\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set\n\n$$B=\\left\\{ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: y=x+1 \\right\\}$$\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee2c580c-5386-5e06-9b58-e9e1dd398cd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6853 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7f009ee8-4a5e-5da7-b539-b2991cbd9d04" :step/math-academy-id 41645 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119500 :question/id #uuid "d2cb7f5f-ad04-5e04-b2da-0f9b555442a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275389}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "a7eb434d-3210-5f2f-b5ec-18781d0fa02d" :step/math-academy-id 41646 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119501 :question/id #uuid "d0d76105-c49e-5cf8-af57-bd74989e895b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10977"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376054}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "911d42c3-5fd0-515e-abcd-cfaab6aa6926" :step/math-academy-id 41660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119502 :question/id #uuid "d2199de1-4bfe-5589-95dd-f9bb7c339437" :question/math-academy-id "e-10980"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376068}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "8b4b4898-6c15-52d9-96fe-d57251b08369" :step/math-academy-id 41667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is $$\\mathbf{d}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] - [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] .$$ This means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is $$\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0bb214-7f56-557a-82f7-baeef815457e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6859 :tutorial/title "Lines as Subspaces of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "124c571f-28f0-56fa-a68d-76ef241f4618" :step/math-academy-id 41674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:*\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form\n\n$$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] = [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 4x-2y\\end{aligned}], \\qquad x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}.$$\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above.\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\mathbf{v}=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$ If we compute their sum, we get $$\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, $\\mathbf{u}=[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$ Then, for any scalar $c$ we have $$\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$ which satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3fcbfc6c-e5f6-5344-8ddd-779b625a03ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6860 :tutorial/title "Planes as Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "2d8c8e20-696b-5df3-beaf-a78962ce0420" :step/math-academy-id 41675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120887}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120047}]}
